---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import diagram from '../assets/pov/three-act-marketing-journey.png';
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
---

<PageLayout
	title="My POV · Aaron Amerling"
	description="Marketing has three acts. A marketing philosophy built on story structure and the Hero's Journey, from Aaron Amerling."
>
	<div class="wrap section-lg prose">
		<p class="eyebrow reveal">My POV</p>
		<h1 class="reveal">Marketing has three acts.</h1>

		<p class="reveal">I came to marketing through writing.</p>
		<p class="reveal">
			I wrote for television. I've written songs and musicals. I've spent a lot of my life
			thinking about characters, motivation, conflict, pacing and, most importantly, what makes
			someone care enough to keep watching.
		</p>
		<p class="reveal">Eventually, I realized I was applying the same thinking to marketing.</p>
		<p class="reveal">Because marketing isn't really about messages.</p>
		<p class="reveal lede"><strong>It's about movement.</strong></p>
	</div>

	<div class="wrap section-lg diagram-block reveal">
		<Image src={diagram} alt="The Three-Act Marketing Journey: how marketing, story structure, and the Hero's Journey align" />
		<p class="mono caption">The three-act marketing journey. How marketing, story structure and the Hero's Journey align.</p>
	</div>

	<div class="wrap prose">
		<h2 class="reveal">Act I: The Buyer</h2>
		<p class="reveal">Every story starts by understanding the protagonist.</p>
		<p class="reveal">In marketing, that's the buyer.</p>
		<p class="reveal">
			Who are they? What do they want? What problem are they actually trying to solve? What do
			they believe? What would have to happen for them to see their situation differently?
		</p>
		<p class="reveal">That's where strategy starts.</p>
		<p class="reveal">Then comes the first turning point.</p>

		<h3 class="reveal">AHA</h3>
		<p class="reveal">The moment the buyer sees something they didn't see before.</p>
		<p class="reveal beat"><em>That's my problem.</em></p>
		<p class="reveal beat"><em>That's why this isn't working.</em></p>
		<p class="reveal beat"><em>That's what I need.</em></p>
		<p class="reveal">The AHA is the transition from Act I to Act II.</p>
		<p class="reveal">
			In the language of the Hero's Journey, it's the <strong>Call to Adventure</strong>, the
			moment the protagonist encounters something that pulls them out of their ordinary world and
			toward something new.
		</p>

		<h2 class="reveal">Act II: The Marketing Journey</h2>
		<p class="reveal">Now the story moves.</p>
		<p class="reveal">
			Marketing's job is to take the buyer from recognition toward action. Every interaction
			should move them somewhere, toward understanding, confidence, desire and ultimately a
			decision.
		</p>
		<p class="reveal">The question isn't just:</p>
		<p class="reveal beat"><em>What should we say?</em></p>
		<p class="reveal">It's:</p>
		<p class="reveal lede"><strong>What does this person need to understand next?</strong></p>
		<p class="reveal">
			The buyer encounters information, proof, objections, possibilities and choices. The best
			marketing doesn't simply repeat a message. It creates momentum.
		</p>
		<p class="reveal">Eventually, they make a purchase.</p>
		<p class="reveal">But that's not the end of the marketing journey.</p>
		<p class="reveal">It's not even the beginning of the product experience.</p>

		<h3 class="reveal">Activation</h3>
		<p class="reveal">
			<strong
				>Activation is when the customer actually uses the thing they bought and experiences its
				value.</strong
			>
		</p>
		<p class="reveal">You can buy a car and never drive it.</p>
		<p class="reveal">You can sign up for a digital service and never use it.</p>
		<p class="reveal">You can create a social media account and never make your first post.</p>
		<p class="reveal">You've acquired the product, but you haven't activated it.</p>
		<p class="reveal">
			Activation is the moment the customer crosses from <strong
				>owning the product to experiencing the product.</strong
			>
		</p>
		<p class="reveal">That's the transition from Act II to Act III.</p>

		<h2 class="reveal">Act III: The Product Experience</h2>
		<p class="reveal">This is where the promise gets tested.</p>
		<p class="reveal lede"><strong>The product is the payoff.</strong></p>
		<p class="reveal">The customer uses it. It solves the problem. Something changes.</p>
		<p class="reveal">And this is where the Hero's Journey comes full circle.</p>
		<p class="reveal">
			The hero returns to the ordinary world, but they aren't the same person who left it. They
			bring back the <strong>elixir</strong>, the thing they learned or gained on the journey.
		</p>
		<p class="reveal">That's what a great product should do.</p>
		<p class="reveal">The customer returns to their life, but their life is better because of the experience.</p>
		<p class="reveal">And when that happens, something powerful occurs.</p>
		<p class="reveal">They tell someone about it.</p>
		<p class="reveal">They become a repeat customer.</p>
		<p class="reveal">They recommend it.</p>
		<p class="reveal">They become an advocate.</p>
		<p class="reveal">The story that began with a problem ends with a transformation.</p>
	</div>
</PageLayout>

<style>
	.lede {
		font-size: 1.3rem;
	}
	.beat {
		padding-left: 1.2em;
		border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
		color: var(--muted);
	}
	.diagram-block {
		padding-top: 0;
	}
	.diagram-block :global(img) {
		width: 100%;
		height: auto;
		border: 1px solid var(--rule);
		display: block;
	}
	.diagram-block .caption {
		margin-top: 1em;
		color: var(--muted);
		font-size: 12px;
	}
</style>
